facts:
An **axis of symmetry** is a line you can fold along so the two halves land exactly on top of each other.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[thick] (-1.8,-1.1)--(1.8,-1.1)--(1.0,1.1)--(-1.0,1.1)--cycle;
\draw[dashed,red] (0,-1.7)--(0,1.7);
\node[above] at (0,1.7) {axis of symmetry};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The **order of rotational symmetry** is how many times a shape matches its starting position in one full $360^{\circ}$ turn.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[thick] (-1.5,-1)--(0.5,-1)--(0.5,0)--(1.5,0)--(1.5,1)--(-0.5,1)--(-0.5,0)--(-1.5,0)--cycle;
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\fill[red] (1.5,0) circle (3.5pt);
\node[below] at (0,-1.3) {start};
\begin{scope}[xshift=5cm]
\draw[thick] (-1.5,-1)--(0.5,-1)--(0.5,0)--(1.5,0)--(1.5,1)--(-0.5,1)--(-0.5,0)--(-1.5,0)--cycle;
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\fill[red] (-1.5,0) circle (3.5pt);
\node[below] at (0,-1.3) {after $180^{\circ}$};
\end{scope}
\draw[->,thick] (2.0,1.5) to[bend left=25] (3.0,1.5);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **graph** has line symmetry about the $y$-axis if reflecting it in the $y$-axis leaves it unchanged.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-2.6,0)--(2.6,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-2.4)--(0,2.6) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[dashed,red] (0,-2.2)--(0,2.3);
\draw[thick,blue] (-2.0,1.8)--(0,-1.2)--(2.0,1.8);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A graph has **half-turn symmetry** about the origin if a $180^{\circ}$ turn about the origin leaves it unchanged. This does not always mean its rotational symmetry has order exactly $2$: a circle centred at the origin is unchanged by every rotation.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-2.6,0)--(2.6,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-2.6)--(0,2.6) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[<->,thick,blue,domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\x,samples=60] plot ({\x},{0.8*\x*\x*\x-2.4*\x});
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\node[above right] at (0,0) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
How many axes of symmetry does this non-equilateral isosceles triangle have?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (0,2)--(-1.2,-1)--(1.2,-1)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Fold along the line through the top vertex and the midpoint of the base. The two halves match exactly. No other fold makes them match, so there is $1$ axis of symmetry.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (0,2)--(-1.2,-1)--(1.2,-1)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,-1.35)--(0,2.35);
\path (0.15,1.5) node[right] {axis of symmetry};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
What is the order of rotational symmetry of this regular hexagon?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (0:1.4)--(60:1.4)--(120:1.4)--(180:1.4)--(240:1.4)--(300:1.4)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Rotate the hexagon about its centre. It matches its starting position at $60^{\circ}$, $120^{\circ}$, $180^{\circ}$, $240^{\circ}$, $300^{\circ}$ and $360^{\circ}$.

There are $6$ matching positions in one full turn, so the order of rotational symmetry is $6$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (0:1.4)--(60:1.4)--(120:1.4)--(180:1.4)--(240:1.4)--(300:1.4)--cycle;
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\draw[dotted] (0,0)--(0:1.4);
\draw[dotted] (0,0)--(60:1.4);
\draw[->] (0:0.65) arc[start angle=0,end angle=60,radius=0.65];
\path (30:0.95) node {$60^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
State the number of lines of symmetry and the order of rotational symmetry of this parallelogram. It is neither a rectangle nor a rhombus.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (-1.8,-0.8)--(1,-0.8)--(1.8,0.8)--(-1,0.8)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
No fold line makes the halves match, so there are $0$ lines of symmetry.

A $180^{\circ}$ turn about the centre swaps opposite vertices and leaves the shape unchanged. It matches at $180^{\circ}$ and $360^{\circ}$, so its rotational symmetry has order $2$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (-1.8,-0.8)--(1,-0.8)--(1.8,0.8)--(-1,0.8)--cycle;
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\path (-1.8,-0.8) node[below left] {$A$};
\path (1,-0.8) node[below right] {$B$};
\path (1.8,0.8) node[above right] {$C$};
\path (-1,0.8) node[above left] {$D$};
\path (0,0) node[above] {centre};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The half-turn swaps $A$ with $C$ and $B$ with $D$.

